# list

#A list is: Ordered, mutable collection

'''Mutable : Can change.

Example: nums = [10,20,30]
nums[0] = 100

Result: [100,20,30]
'''


# tuple
#A tuple is: Ordered, immutable collection

'''Internal Advantage

Because immutable:

Python optimizes memory.

Tuple faster than list.

Safer.'''

'''
Tuple Packing/Unpacking :

point = (10,20)
x,y = point

Result:
x = 10
y = 20

'''

# set
#A set is: Unordered, mutable collection of unique elements
'''
Internal Working

Set uses: Hash Table

(Important concept)
Python converts value into: hash number

Like locker system.

Instead of searching linearly:

Python jumps directly.

Example:

5 in s

Python:

Not: check one-by-one

Instead: jump to hash location

So lookup: O(1)

Very fast.

Example
s = {1,2,3}

print(2 in s)

Fast lookup.
'''

# dictionary
#A dictionary is: Unordered, mutable collection of key-value pairs

'''Internal Working
Think locker analogy.

Example:

Dictionary:

{
   "name":"Arsul"
}

Python hashes: "name" to a number.

Example:

locker 52

So when searching:

Python directly jumps:

locker 52

No scanning.

That is why:

Lookup = O(1)
'''